├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ scarcity_aware_regret_and_tighter_queueing                                                │
│ patch_description        │ This edit improves assignment quality in two coordinated ways.                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ First, it makes the regret ordering explicitly scarcity-aware. The current construction   │
│                          │ phase prioritizes tasks mostly by the gap between their best and second-best placements,  │
│                          │ with a demand boost. That still allows flexible tasks to consume scarce low-load server   │
│                          │ capacity too early. The new priority computes how many candidate servers remain “safe”    │
│                          │ for each source under the current partial assignment: below the soft-utilization target   │
│                          │ and with modest queue-cost increase. Sources with fewer safe options get boosted, while   │
│                          │ highly flexible sources are slightly deprioritized. This better preserves rare good       │
│                          │ placements for harder tasks.                                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Second, it slightly strengthens queue/activation tradeoffs by using a more M/M/1-like     │
│                          │ queue term in server_state_cost and a somewhat more latency-sensitive path set. This is   │
│                          │ intended to reduce placements that look cheap initially but create avoidable queueing     │
│                          │ pressure, while still keeping energy competitive.                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ These are targeted changes only within the evolve block and preserve the required output  │
│                          │ format and validity behavior.                                                             │
