                        Patch Metadata - Gen 49/50 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/10                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ compact_pareto_beam                                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ This redesign keeps the efficient “few servers, many sources” perspective from the prior  │
│                          │ programs, but changes the optimization pattern in two important ways.                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ First, instead of keeping either a single route per server or a very large set of         │
│                          │ weighted shortest-path trees, it builds a compact candidate library per `(source,         │
│                          │ server)`. For each server, several server-rooted Dijkstra trees are computed under        │
│                          │ different latency-energy mixtures, then for each source only a tiny non-dominated subset  │
│                          │ is retained: usually the best route plus at most one meaningfully different tradeoff      │
│                          │ route. This follows the observed recommendation that compact path diversity is more       │
│                          │ useful than broad blind expansion.                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Second, assignment is no longer a single one-pass greedy decision. The algorithm          │
│                          │ generates several greedy plans using different source orderings and scoring biases,       │
│                          │ evaluates them with a consistent exact surrogate objective, and then improves the best    │
│                          │ plans with a fast local-search repair stage that tries single-source reassignment/path    │
│                          │ swaps when they reduce total latency-energy score. This is different from the earlier     │
│                          │ direct tree scan or broad parameter grid: it is a compact route-set + multi-start greedy  │
│                          │ + hill-climbing architecture.                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Key ideas:                                                                                │
│                          │ - Few servers => precompute from servers, not from sources.                               │
│                          │ - Many sources => compress route options aggressively.                                    │
│                          │ - Server queueing is the dominant coupling term, so use exact server load bookkeeping and │
│                          │ a local improvement pass.                                                                 │
│                          │ - Energy and latency are balanced both in route generation and in assignment, but         │
│                          │ candidate explosion is avoided.                                                           │
